<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Virarajendra Chola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1063">1063</a>–1070</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Virarajendra <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-dynasty">Chola dynasty</a> of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, ruling from 1063 to 1070, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra Chola I</a>. He fought repeated wars against the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, defeating them at Kudal Sangamam, and campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. His inscriptions record a naval expedition about 1068 that conquered Kadaram (Kedah) and restored its ruler, who had sought Chola help.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/virarajendra-chola.jpg" alt="Virarajendra Chola" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Virarajendra_Chola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Virarajendra spent most of his life serving his two elder brothers, Rajadhiraja Chola I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-ii">Rajendra Chola II</a>, who reigned before him. Rajadhiraja posted him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> early in his own reign, and under Rajendra II he was Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraiyur">Uraiyur</a>. He fought the Western Chalukyas before he became king, at the Battle of Mudakkaru under the heir Rajamahendra. The three brothers ruled in turn for 16 to 20 years in all, while the Sinhalese, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> and the Chera Perumals tried to break free of Chola control.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Early in his reign he killed the kings of Pottapi and of the Chera Perumal country and put down a rising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> princes. When the Western Chalukya prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a> attacked the capital, Gangaikonda Cholapuram, he returned to defend it. His inscriptions claim that he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/someshvara-i">Someshvara I</a> at least five times. Someshvara named Kudal Sangamam for a battle on 10 September 1067, but his army never came; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> army waited more than a month and set up a pillar of victory on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tungabhadra-river">Tungabhadra River</a>.</p><p>He then won a battle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> near Visaiyavadai, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayawada">Vijayawada</a>, and placed the Eastern Chalukya prince Vijayaditya VI on the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> the Sinhalese king Vijayabahu, with ships and soldiers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, kept stirring revolts in the northern provinces held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>. When a succession war broke out among the Western Chalukyas, Virarajendra received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a> at his court, recognised him as king and gave him a daughter in marriage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He founded a school for the study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, Sastras and grammar with a hostel for students, and a hospital named Virasolan; the Tamil Buddhist grammar Virasoliyam was written by Buddhamitra in his reign. A devotee of Sivan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chidambaram">Chidambaram</a>, he also endowed temples of other faiths. His queen was Arumolinangai, and his daughter Rajasundari married a prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-ganga-dynasty">Eastern Ganga dynasty</a> and was the mother of Anantavarman Chodaganga Deva. He died early in 1070 and was succeeded by his son Athirajendra Chola.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Virarajendra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1025 the Chola king Rajendra Chola I launched a great naval raid that sacked Srivijaya and a string of its ports, including Kadaram (Kedah) and Malaiyur, and captured its ruler Sangrama Vijayatunggavarman; a further Chola expedition followed around 1068 under <b>Virarajendra</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Someshvara’s forces were defeated at Kudalasangamam in the 1060s by <b>Virarajendra Chola</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
